+# R2 independent money-path validation
+
+Reviewed source: `4f9fba2fd95d6fd8057c27d56cfad0297b9f06ce`, 23 September 2026. Scope: F-1 through F-6. Read-only specialist review; no repository edits, live provider request, signer call, broadcast, funding, deployment, or credentials access. This report is not a financial readiness approval.
+
+## F-3: reproduced through the actual adapter
+
+`f3-eleven-builds.mts` calls the repository's `epochCostForecast`, `safeJupiterBuild`, and `bindNativeCost`. It supplies a valid encoded legacy Jupiter route and mocked RPC mint/account responses, then walks one buyback and ten purchases, each with a new output ATA, deducting each finalized net fee plus output rent from the available cost account. The mock has no signing or sending method. The last burn's fee allowance is also checked. This is an executable adapter/cash sufficiency test, not a full worker/database or live-provider test.
+
+Run from the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+node --import tsx scripts/audit/f3-eleven-builds.mts ../f3-eleven-builds-results.json
+```
+
+Result: PASS (the old implementation's expected failure was reproduced). See `f3-eleven-builds-results.json` for every transaction's balance and required cash.
+
+| Forecast | Actual fee per transaction | Completed swap/buyback builds | Cash before final rejected purchase | Shortfall | Cash after 11 swaps + burn when passing |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| Current 23,632,080 | 5,000 | 10 / 11 | 3,189,280 | 989,280 | — |
+| Current 23,632,080 | 100,000 | 10 / 11 | 2,239,280 | 1,939,280 | — |
+| Plus one WSOL float: 25,671,360 | 5,000 | 11 / 11 | — | — | 3,179,280 |
+| Plus one WSOL float: 25,671,360 | 100,000 | 11 / 11 | — | — | 2,039,280 |
+
+All amounts are lamports. Constants: fee cap 100,000; ATA rent 2,039,280. Current forecast reserves 12 fees and 11 net ATA rents. The adapter requires a temporary WSOL rent in addition to the output ATA rent, so each all-new-ATA build demands 4,178,560 of current cost-account cash. Net rent accounting correctly excludes the returned WSOL rent, but admission still needs that float. Under these assumptions `(size + 2) * maxFee + (size + 2) * rent` suffices. This does not fund unbounded failed retries, establish current Jupiter compatibility, or repair expiry recovery.
+
+The desired repository regression should use the above eleven actual adapter builds and cost-account depletion, then exercise `runIntent`/`Engine.apply` with original finalized outcome fixtures in the isolated test DB. Assert all eleven financial intents finalize, burn retains its fee cap, exact acquired units are credited once, and leftover cost is returned only after all obligations finalize. Replace the current test that merely asserts the forecast formula's literal output. Include existing-output ATA, all-new output ATA, max-fee, and finalized-failure costs.
+
+## Remaining blocker validation and evidence needed
+
+| Finding | Independently validated | What can resolve it |
+|---|---|---|
+| F-1 | Confirmed in `provenance.ts:86-120` and `jupiter-tokens.ts:7-11`. The built-in Jupiter path attaches permanent methodology/category/window blockers, returns before chain verification, and never supplies `metrics`, which `complete` requires. The custom path is an exact complete-catalogue contract with <=180-second freshness and exact integer supply/price arithmetic. | A product/provider decision is required: procure/build and review a complete evidence service satisfying the existing policy, or explicitly revise policy with the user. Do not convert undocumented reported `mcap`, tags, or `stats24h` into verified eligibility. Need catalogue completeness contract, supply methodology and comparable rank basis, mint/pool identity, explicit category coverage, complete timestamped 24h USD volume, signed/versioned or hash-bound provenance, and freshness/coverage failure behavior. The repo does not supply that service. |
+| F-2 | Adapter accepts only decoded legacy `route` with one route-plan hop. Both discriminator prefixes reported by R2 decode to `null` using the installed parser; this was executed locally. The audit's live HTTP responses were not re-fetched by this reviewer. The locally valid fixture used for F-3 proves only the accepted legacy shape. | Need a read-only authenticated build capture for the actual treasury and proposed output/budget (key must remain secret), including timestamp, query parameters, full raw instructions, lookup tables, route plan and compute-budget instructions. Decode using current primary IDL/schema and bind accounts, amounts, mint identity, minimum output, approved programs and fee limits. Decide whether policy permits newly documented route variants. A safe compatibility fix may not blindly accept unknown discriminators or discard the direct-route policy. No live signing is needed for this evidence. |
+| F-4 | Confirmed: `SolanaChain.inspect` returns `expired`, runner persists `needs_review`, worker's in-flight check blocks new work. `expired_verified` is read but never written. `recoverIntent` only re-inspects. Resolving incidents does not retire the intent/attempt or release reservation. Locally built transactions also discard remote compute-budget price instructions. | Design and test authenticated, idempotent retirement that proves the original signature absent after validity expiry on independent archival history providers, persists evidence, transitions attempt terminally and releases only unused reservations. Preserve original bytes and ambiguity; never substitute operator opinion for finalized/absence evidence. Explicit recovery semantics are needed for landed but unaccounted transactions. Independently bound any accepted priority fee. |
+| F-5 | Stored 100-row fixture census executed: zero `payout + keep + WSOL` rows; two keep rows are `claim` with amount 0 (one WSOL with claimed 0.525022996). Missing signatures on `perp` and `sweep` make the existing strict `EmberClient.payouts` reject the full window; reproduced locally. Ingestion permanently advances its cursor and `Engine.ingest` ignores existing transfer IDs, so later matching evidence cannot remove quarantine. | Capture the real configured Keep-it pool's before/after payout window and corresponding finalized on-chain transaction, decoded sender, recipient, mint, amount, fees and instruction identity. Obtain provider meaning of `claim` versus `payout`, `claimed` versus `amount`, payout destination/unwrap semantics and any aggregation. The historical global fixture does not prove what a project's Keep-it pool does. Tolerantly skip irrelevant malformed rows without admitting them as funding; add append-only, idempotent linked re-attribution after exact independent evidence is available. Do not loosen the predicate to make the fixture pass. |
+| F-6 | Confirmed code mechanism: any non-null WSOL source ATA account is rejected before building, including a system-owned prefund. `waiting_for_route` is absent from the new-epoch guard. The WSOL close returns pre-existing lamports to the payer, while settlement calculates input from net payer delta; residual returned lamports reduce observed input, causing `Engine.apply` rejection. This landing/race trigger was not executed against a chain. | Design safe handling separately for valid existing zero-balance WSOL accounts, existing token balances and system-owned prefunding. Derive exact consumed input from verified original instructions and WSOL account balance evidence, not only payer net delta. Prevent new epochs while committed work is known unexecutable. Preserve accounting for donations and externally deposited WSOL; no silent close, confiscation, revenue attribution or credit fabrication. Add original-message and pre/post account fixtures for each case and a local-validator execution when available. |
+
+## Limits and conclusion
+
+F-3 is a independently reproduced defect, not merely a forecast assertion. F-1 is an external evidence/product dependency; F-2 is an adapter/provider compatibility gate; F-4/F-6 need lifecycle/accounting changes; F-5 requires real pool evidence before choosing attribution semantics. Completing public-site fixes or adding one rent to the forecast cannot establish funded-pilot readiness. Keep financial execution disabled while these gates remain unresolved.
